{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":1,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1563838001,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDU6SXNzdWU0NzEzOTMyNjc=","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3551","origin":"github"},"title":"run: seems to incorrectly handle relative paths if ran within subdirectory","message":"There could be more oddities or just that my eyes are sore already (couldn't reliably reproduce for a bit and then it \"worked\").  Calling datalad run with output relative path being somewhere `../` causes two kinds of (wrong) behavior when output path points to a subdataset:\n\n1. immediate subdataset\n```\n/tmp/topds \u003e cd /tmp; sudo rm -rf topds; datalad create /tmp/topds; cd /tmp/topds/; mkdir sub; touch sub/input; datalad save -m cleanup; datalad create -d . output; cd sub; datalad run --explicit --input ../sub/input --output ../output md5sum '{inputs}'                        \n[INFO   ] Creating a new annex repo at /tmp/topds \ncreate(ok): /tmp/topds (dataset)                                                                                \nadd(ok): sub/input (file)\nsave(ok): . (dataset)\naction summary:\n  add (ok: 1)\n  save (ok: 1)\n[INFO   ] Creating a new annex repo at /tmp/topds/output \ncreate(ok): output (dataset)                                                                                    \naction summary:\n  add (ok: 2)\n  create (ok: 1)\n  save (ok: 1)\n[INFO   ] Making sure inputs are available (this may take some time) \n[WARNING] path not associated with any dataset [get(/tmp)] \nget(impossible): .. (directory) [path not associated with any dataset]\n[INFO   ] == Command start (output follows) ===== \nd41d8cd98f00b204e9800998ecf8427e  input\n[INFO   ] == Command exit (modification check follows) ===== \naction summary:\n  get (impossible: 1, notneeded: 1)\n\n```\nso seems to work but see the WARNING above -- `..` seems to be taken from the top of the dataset?\n\n2. when output points to sub-subdataset, it just crashes:\n\n```shell\n/tmp/topds \u003e cd /tmp; sudo rm -rf topds; datalad create /tmp/topds; cd /tmp/topds/; mkdir sub; touch sub/input; datalad save -m cleanup; datalad create -d . -c text2git output; datalad create -d output/sub; cd sub; datalad run --explicit --input ../sub/input --output ../output/sub bash -c 'md5sum {inputs} \u003e {outputs}/checksum' \n[INFO   ] Creating a new annex repo at /tmp/topds \ncreate(ok): /tmp/topds (dataset)                                                                                \nadd(ok): sub/input (file)\nsave(ok): . (dataset)\naction summary:\n  add (ok: 1)\n  save (ok: 1)\n[INFO   ] Creating a new annex repo at /tmp/topds/output \ncreate(ok): output (dataset)                                                                                    \n[INFO   ] Running procedure cfg_text2git \n[INFO   ] == Command start (output follows) ===== \n[INFO   ] == Command exit (modification check follows) ===== \naction summary:\n  add (ok: 2)\n  create (ok: 1)\n  save (ok: 1)\n[INFO   ] Creating a new annex repo at /tmp/topds/output/sub \ncreate(ok): . (dataset)                                                                                         \n[INFO   ] Making sure inputs are available (this may take some time) \n[ERROR  ] '/tmp/topds/sub/../output/sub' does not start with '/tmp/topds/output' [__init__.py:relative_to:1077] (ValueError) \n```\n\nso if first one just feels that there is treatment of relative paths as there was `--dataset` argument -- from the top of it, in the 2nd case feels like some normalization of the path is due before asking pathlib's `PosixPath.relative_to`","files":null}]}